        <div class="situation-card__label">{item.label}</div>
        <div class="situation-card__title">{item.title}</div>
        <p>{item.body}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <nav class="jump-nav" aria-label="Article sections">
    <p class="jump-nav__label">Jump to section</p>
    <ul class="jump-nav__list">
      {jumpLinks.map((item) => (
        <li><a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.label}</a></li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="this-week">What is open this week</h2>

  <p>
    Students keep asking questions like "I got a rank of 20,000, which course is best?", "can BiPC students get BSc Agriculture with a 30,000 rank?" and "do I need NEET for nursing in Telangana?" Most answers online are undated. This table is dated 30 September 2026 and shows where each door stands right now.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Door</th>
          <th>Where it stands</th>
          <th>What to do</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {openRows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Door"><strong>{row.door}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Where it stands">{row.status}</td>
            <td data-label="What to do">{row.todo}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Dates come from university notices and counselling reports and can move with a court order or a revised schedule. Two sources gave different Andhra Pradesh final-phase dates (17 September original, 23 September revised); the 23 September date is the one two reports agree on.
  </p>

  <h2 id="two-states">Nursing: one student, two states, opposite rules</h2>

  <p>
    Most pages on this topic say "BSc Nursing needs NEET" or "BSc Nursing needs no NEET". Both statements are wrong for one of your two states.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Item</th>
          <th>Andhra Pradesh (Dr NTR UHS)</th>
          <th>Telangana (KNRUHS)</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {stateRows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Item"><strong>{row.item}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Andhra Pradesh (Dr NTR UHS)">{row.ap}</td>
            <td data-label="Telangana (KNRUHS)">{row.ts}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    The Telangana numbers can be checked with a calculator. The exam had 84,954 candidates appear in the agriculture and pharmacy stream. Half of that is 42,477, exactly the general cut-off rank in the prospectus.
  </p>
  <p>
    Sixty percent is 50,972, and fifty-five percent is 46,724, matching the other two lines. The percentile line is counted on everyone who appeared, not on those who qualified.
  </p>

  <div class="stat-grid">
    <div class="stat-card">
      <div class="stat-card__num">73,294</div>
      <p>candidates qualified in TG EAPCET agriculture and pharmacy (86 percent of 84,954 who appeared)</p>
    </div>
    <div class="stat-card">
      <div class="stat-card__num">42,477</div>
      <p>the last rank inside the general nursing line, 50th percentile of those who appeared</p>
    </div>
    <div class="stat-card">
      <div class="stat-card__num">30,817</div>
      <p>qualified candidates who rank below that line (73,294 minus 42,477) and cannot apply under the general rule</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      "Qualified" and "eligible" are different words. A Telangana student with a rank of 55,000 qualified in EAPCET and still misses the general nursing line. That student needs a second lane on the same day, not after the merit list appears.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Andhra Pradesh notice asks for candidates who have appeared in NEET UG 2026. Read the prospectus for any percentile line, and remember that about 20 lakh candidates appeared nationally, so the state merit list decides your position, not the national rank.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="callout callout--amber">
    <p class="callout__eyebrow">What this means for you</p>
    <ul class="callout__list">
      <li>Live in Andhra Pradesh and want nursing or the veterinary VCI quota: sit NEET UG. It costs little and keeps both open.</li>
      <li>Live in Telangana: your EAPCET rank is the ticket for nursing, and the 42,477 line is the number to compare yours against.</li>
      <li>Moving between states for college adds a local-status problem. Telangana nursing needs four consecutive years of study or residence in the state.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2 id="vet">BVSc and the NEET rule that stops at the state line</h2>

  <p>
    Veterinary is often filed under "needs NEET". That is only partly true, and the split shows up in the seat type.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Seat type</th>
          <th>Rule</th>
          <th>NEET needed</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {vetRows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Seat type"><strong>{row.seat}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Rule">{row.rule}</td>
            <td data-label="NEET needed">{row.neet}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    The Telangana rows are from the PVNRTVU admissions note for 2026-27. The Andhra Pradesh row is a secondary reading. Seat counts for Andhra Pradesh differ between sources, so use the SVVU bulletin for your year.
  </p>

  <p>
    Veterinary is also a multi-stage competition. First a small state pool at EAPCET, then a licence, then the slow work of building a rural or urban clinical practice. The state BVSc pool is 71 to 185 seats in Telangana as listed, and 28 in fisheries, so rank is not a formality.
  </p>

  <h2 id="doors">Which door your rank opens</h2>

  <p>
    This table sorts each door by the gate it uses, not by how glamorous it sounds. Save it and check each row against the official notice before you pay a fee.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Door</th>
          <th>Entry gate</th>
          <th>What rank means here</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {doorRows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Door"><strong>{row.door}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Entry gate">{row.gate}</td>
            <td data-label="What rank means here">{row.rank}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="seat-maths">Seats per qualifier, computed</h2>

  <p>
    Here is the arithmetic behind the BiPC counselling numbers. It explains why pharmacy is easy to enter and why the smaller pools feel harder.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Stage</th>
          <th>Numbers</th>
          <th>The sum</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {seatRows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Stage"><strong>{row.stage}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Numbers">{row.numbers}</td>
            <td data-label="The sum">{row.sum}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Qualifier counts come from results reports. Seat counts come from counselling reports and are for phase 1, so later rounds change them. They cover pharmacy, biotechnology and related BiPC-stream seats, not agriculture or veterinary, which run separate counselling.
  </p>

  <p>
    The pattern is simple. The BiPC pharmacy pool is seat-rich, so almost every registrant in Andhra Pradesh got a seat, and 826 were vacant after round one. The scarce things are the top college names and the government seats in nursing, veterinary and agriculture.
  </p>
  <p>
    If your rank is in the tens of thousands, do not spend the year chasing a college you cannot reach. Choose from what is open and put your effort into what you build alongside the degree.
  </p>

  <h2 id="lanes">Bio student career options, six lanes</h2>

  <p>
    These lanes are ordered by realistic growth headroom, meaning how far income and independence can stretch with skill and proof, not by prestige or exam difficulty. That is a judgement, not a rule.
  </p>
  <p>
    Life science plus data leads because the skill travels across employers and can be sold remotely. Nursing sits fifth because the ladder is public and steady, but its private-sector ceiling is capped.
  </p>

  <div class="lane-grid">
    {lanes.map((lane) => (
      <div class:list={['lane-card', `lane-card--${lane.tone}`]}>
        <span class="lane-card__badge">{lane.badge}</span>
        <div class="lane-card__title">{lane.title}</div>
        <p class="lane-card__entry"><strong>How you enter:</strong> {lane.entry}</p>
        <p>{lane.take}</p>
        <div class="lane-row lane-row--fit"><span>Best for</span> {lane.best}</div>
        <div class="lane-row lane-row--watch"><span>Watch out</span> {lane.watch}</div>
        <p class="lane-card__read">
          Read next: <a href={lane.read.href}>{lane.read.label}</a>
          {lane.read2 && (<>{' '}or <a href={lane.read2.href}>{lane.read2.label}</a></>)}
        </p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
    Several of these lanes carry competition at two stages, not one. Veterinary and agriculture first need a rank in a small pool, then a second contest for a scarce government post or a clinic that earns. Nursing has an open seat stage and a separate contest for government posts.
  </p>
  <p>
    If your rank is strong and income ceiling matters most, Lane 1 with a data skill often beats a low-fee private seat in Lane 5. If stability matters most, a government nursing seat can beat a plain BSc.
  </p>
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      <strong>Scalable ownership inside these lanes:</strong> an owned veterinary clinic or livestock service, an owned pharmacy, a farm-advisory or agri-input business, a nutrition or lab-testing service, and a consulting or freelance health-data service all give you pricing control and repeat income that a fixed salary never offers. Each needs registration, credibility and a client base, so treat it as a later stage, not a first-year plan.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="fee-pay">Fees against first pay</h2>

  <p>
    The college itself should stay a small share of your total education budget. As a strict planning heuristic, not a law, keep it near 10 percent and treat anything above about 20 percent as a caution that needs course-specific proof. The rest pays for living costs, tools, courses and the upskilling that raises income.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--money">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Route</th>
          <th>Cost</th>
          <th>Early pay signal</th>
          <th>The sum</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {payRows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Route"><strong>{row.route}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Cost">{row.cost}</td>
            <td data-label="Early pay signal">{row.pay}</td>
            <td data-label="The sum">{row.read}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Here is the same heuristic applied to three illustrative family budgets. Replace them with your own number.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Total education budget</th>
          <th>10 percent (benchmark)</th>
          <th>20 percent (caution line)</th>
          <th>What fits</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {budgetRows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Total education budget"><strong>{row.budget}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="10 percent (benchmark)">{row.ten}</td>
            <td data-label="20 percent (caution line)">{row.twenty}</td>
            <td data-label="What fits">{row.fit}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
    Avoid an education loan for a private seat unless the credential is legally required and the fee is small next to realistic early pay. For regulated degrees such as nursing, pharmacy and veterinary you need the formal route. For add-on skills such as data handling, lab documentation or medical writing, try free YouTube and low-cost courses first, and pay only for feedback or a credential an employer values.
  </p>
  <p>
    A certificate alone is weak proof. Work you can show is strong proof.
  </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="myth-rule">Myth against the notice</h2>

